vtsatip - developer notes
=========================

How the plugin works, how it implements SAT>IP and how it talks to VDR. For
usage see README, for credits and license see AUTHORS.

1. Big picture
--------------

    VDR core                          vtsatip                        SAT>IP server
    --------                          -------                        -------------
    cDevice API  <----------------->  cVtSatipDevice  (device.c)
    (tuning, PIDs, filters,               |  tune/PID requests (non-blocking)
     TS packets, CAM, EPG)                v
                                      cVtTuner thread  (tuner.c)  --- RTSP/TCP ---> OPTIONS, SETUP,
                                          |   one RTSP session                       PLAY, TEARDOWN
                                          v
                                      cVtReceiver thread (tuner.c) <-- RTP/RTCP --- UDP unicast,
                                          |   ts packets                            multicast or
                                          v                                         interleaved TCP
                                      ring buffer + software section filters
                                      (device.c, sectionfilter.c)  ---> back to VDR

    cVtServers (servers.c) knows the servers and which device uses which
    frontend; cVtDiscover (discover.c) fills it by UPnP/SSDP;
    cMenuSetupVtSatip / cMenuVtSatipStats (setup.c) is the OSD.

Each VDR device (-d N) is one cVtSatipDevice with its own tuner thread, its
own receiver thread, its own RTSP connection and its own SAT>IP stream, so N
devices need N frontends on the servers.

Threads (per device, plus one global):
  * VDR threads call into cVtSatipDevice (SetChannelDevice, SetPid, ...).
    They never block on the network.
  * Tuner thread (cVtTuner::Action): the only thread that sends RTSP
    requests. Waits on a condition variable for requests, then does the
    minimal RTSP work. Also sends keep-alives (OPTIONS) and rebuilds the session
    after errors.
  * Receiver thread (cVtReceiver::Action): reads RTP and RTCP, strips the RTP
    header, hands whole TS packets to the device. SCHED_FIFO if allowed.
  * Section handler thread (cVtSecHandler): runs the software section filters.
  * Discovery thread (cVtDiscover::Action, one for the plugin).

2. Files
--------

  vtsatip.c        plugin class: command line, SVDRP, setup glue, thread start
  common.h         constants, cVtSatipConfig (all settings), logging macros
  vtconfig.c       parsing of settings (CA IDs, CI slots, SIDs, setup.conf keys)
  device.c/.h      cVtSatipDevice: the cDevice implementation
  tuner.c/.h       cVtTuner (RTSP control) and cVtReceiver (RTP/RTCP input)
  rtsp.c/.h        minimal blocking RTSP client incl. RTP-over-TCP framing
  param.c/.h       channels.conf parameters -> SAT>IP query (from the satip plugin)
  pat.c/.h         PAT parser and PMT PID cache (needed for the CI extension)
  sectionfilter.*  software section filters for VDR's EIT/SDT/... filters
  servers.c/.h     server list, frontend accounting, quirks bookkeeping
  discover.c/.h    SSDP/UPnP discovery
  setup.c/.h       setup menu and statistics page
  tests/           stubs of the VDR headers, a fake SAT>IP server, test programs

3. SAT>IP in a nutshell, and what vtsatip does
----------------------------------------------

SAT>IP (SAT>IP specification, "SAT>IP Protocol Specification" by SES Astra)
streams MPEG-TS over RTP. Control is RTSP, one session = one frontend.

  OPTIONS rtsp://server/                      liveness, also used as keep-alive
  SETUP   rtsp://server/?src=1&freq=..&pol=..&msys=..&mtype=..&sr=..&fec=..&pids=none
            -> Session: ID;timeout=N, com.ses.streamID: S, Transport: ...
  PLAY    rtsp://server/stream=S?<changes>    start, and every later change
  TEARDOWN rtsp://server/stream=S

The query parameters (all from param.c / NormalizeParams in tuner.c):
  src=      satellite position number, from the description of the source in
            sources.conf (leading number), not from VDR's DiSEqC setup
  fe=       force a frontend (option -f / setup menu)
  freq pol msys mtype sr fec ro plts   tuning, DVB-S/S2; for terrestrial and
            cable the matching parameters (bw, tmode, gi, ...)
  pids=     the complete PID list ("none", "all", or "0,16,17,...")
  addpids=, delpids=   incremental PID changes
  x_pmt=, x_ci=        CI extension (see 5)

NormalizeParams brings the query into the order and form vtuner-ng sends it
(src, fe, freq, pol, msys, mtype, sr, fec, ro, plts) and adds fec/plts, because
several servers reject a SETUP without them.

Control flow, which is what makes switching fast (the same as in vtuner-ng):

  first tuning of a device:   OPTIONS, SETUP (pids=none), PLAY (tuning + pids)
  next channel, other transponder:   one PLAY with the new tuning + all PIDs
  next channel, same transponder:    nothing (lock stays); only addpids/delpids
  PID change:                        PLAY addpids/delpids at once, no timer
  idle (disconnect-idle on):         TEARDOWN, frontend is free for others

PIDs 0,16,17,18,19,20 (PAT, NIT, SDT, EIT, ...) are always requested. "Lock" is
declared when the first real TS packet (PID != 0x1FFF) arrives, not by waiting
for RTCP. Packets which arrive after a retune request but belong to the old
transponder are dropped (accept flag + OnRetune(), which flushes the buffers).

Signal values come from the RTCP APP packet "SES1" (RFC 3550 type 204): its text
has "tuner=feID,level,lock,quality,..."; level 0..255 and quality 0..15 are
scaled to VDR's 0..100 / dBm. Lost and reordered packets are counted from the
RTP sequence numbers (cVtReceiver::ProcessRtp). The same function times every
packet (TrackTiming): the data rate and the longest pause between two packets
over 2 s windows and since the (re)tune. A stall that is going on counts at
once; the first packet after a retune does not (ResetSequence). These values
are meant for finding out whether a video underrun comes from late delivery.

Transports (SETUP "Transport" header):
  unicast UDP     RTP/AVP;unicast;client_port=a-b
  multicast       RTP/AVP;multicast  -> the server answers with destination,
                  port and optionally source (SSM); the receiver joins the group
  TCP             RTP/AVP/TCP;unicast;interleaved=0-1 -> RTP and RTCP arrive as
                  "$"-framed data on the RTSP connection. cVtRtsp::Transact
                  and cVtRtsp::Pump share one buffer under one mutex: the
                  tuner thread reads while it waits for a response, the receiver
                  thread otherwise.
A server which refuses or ignores multicast/TCP is remembered (noMulticastM,
noTcpM per server) and gets plain UDP unicast.

4. Server quirks, handled generically
-------------------------------------
There is no table of device names. The tuner reacts to what a server does:
  * refused request and the session ID has leading zeros -> retry without them
    (cVtRtsp::Play)
  * addpids/delpids refused -> send the complete list from now on (fullPidListM)
  * retune refused inside a session -> TEARDOWN and a fresh SETUP/PLAY
  * 503 on SETUP or PLAY (no free frontend) -> cVtTuner calls
    cVtTunerSink::OnServerBusy; the device marks the server busy for 15 s
    (cVtServers::MarkBusy) and moves to another server (Reassign)
  * lock not reported -> irrelevant, lock comes from the first TS packet

5. CI/CAM through the server (x_pmt / x_ci)
-------------------------------------------
Servers with a CI (Digital Devices OctopusNet, minisatip with CA) decrypt in
the server if the stream is set up with x_pmt=<PMT PIDs>&x_ci=<slot>. vtsatip
does not touch VDR's CAM layer for this; the device just claims to have an
internal CAM (HasInternalCam) so VDR does not look for another one.

  1. ProvidesChannel only takes an encrypted channel if one CA ID of the channel
     is in the CA ID list of the device's CI slot (-c / -i / -S), and a server
     with a CI exists (cVtServers::Provides(..., needCi)).
  2. The PMT PID of the service is not known up front. The device feeds the
     stream's PAT into cVtPatParser (CRC32-checked), which yields SID -> PMT PID.
     Results are cached per source/transponder/SID (cVtPmtCache).
  3. cVtTuner::SetPmt(pid) adds it to x_pmt (and to the PID list). Some servers
     only attach the CI to a stream started with x_pmt, so the first time
     the session is restarted once (ciRestart; -Q disables that). Another
     service on the same transponder is just added to the x_pmt list.

6. Interaction with VDR (cDevice)
---------------------------------
  ProvidesSource / ProvidesTransponder / ProvidesChannel
        answer "can this device receive that?" without side effects. They check
        the delivery system (-D), whether a known server has a free frontend of
        that kind and, for sat, the satellite position filter; ProvidesChannel
        also does the CI check and the "already tuned to this transponder"
        logic VDR expects (needsDetachReceivers).
  SetChannelDevice
        builds the SAT>IP parameters (GetTransponderUrlParameters), picks the
        server (cVtServers::Assign), computes x_pmt/x_ci and hands everything
        to cVtTuner::SetChannel. It returns at once, it never waits for lock.
  SetPid / OpenFilter / CloseFilter
        update the wanted PID set of the tuner (see 3). OpenFilter creates a
        cVtSecFilter; EIT & co. are cut out of the TS in software.
  GetTSPacket
        VDR polls this; it returns whole TS packets from the ring buffer that
        the receiver thread (via OnTsData) fills. With the CI active, packets can
        be passed through VDR's cam slot as usual.
  HasLock / SignalStats / SignalStrength / SignalQuality
        from the cVtTuner atomics, fed by RTCP.
  ProvidesEIT, SetPowerSaveMode, MainThreadHook
        EPG scan permission (setup) and release of the stream when idle.
        VDR calls SetPowerSaveMode(true) only from the EPG scanner's pause
        (cDevice::SetPowerSaveIfUnused), so it does not cover a device that is
        simply not needed any more. The plugin's MainThreadHook runs once a
        second (VDR main thread, like SetChannelDevice) and calls
        cVtSatipDevice::IdleCheck: a device that is not receiving and not
        occupied, and was not tuned for -I seconds (default 20), goes through
        SetPowerSaveMode(true): tuner Release (TEARDOWN), server binding freed,
        channel forgotten so IsTunedToTransponder() is false and the next
        SetChannel retunes. VDR always calls SetChannelDevice for a switch.
  Setup: SetupParse/SetupStore, the OSD menus use VDR's cMenuSetupPage and
        cOsdMenu; settings in setup.conf override command-line values.
  SVDRP: INFO, SCAN, ATTA, DETA, TRAC (vtsatip.c, SVDRPCommand).

Locking rules worth knowing:
  * cVtTuner::mutexM protects the requests from VDR (wanted params, PIDs,
    PMTs) and is held only briefly; the tuner thread copies what it needs and
    does the network work without it.
  * cVtServers has its own mutex; it is the only place that knows which
    device sits on which server.
  * cVtReceiver::sockMutexM protects the sockets (they are replaced when a stream
    goes multicast); changers go first (changersM) because pthread mutexes are
    not fair and the receive loop would starve them.
  * cVtRtsp::ioM serialises all reads on the RTSP socket (see TCP above).

7. Server discovery (UPnP / SSDP)
---------------------------------
cVtDiscover sends M-SEARCH (ST: urn:ses-com:device:SatIPServer:1) to
239.255.255.250:1900 every 60 s and listens for NOTIFY ssdp:alive/byebye. For a
new LOCATION it fetches the description with a plain HTTP GET, reads
friendlyName, modelName and X_SATIPCAP (e.g. "DVBS2-4,DVBT2-2" = frontends
per system), plus the X-SATIP-RTSP-Port header. cVtServers::Update stores the
server; entries expire with the max-age of the announcement. Manual servers (-s)
win over discovered ones. No XML library: the small tag scanner is in
discover.c.

The datagram handler gets the sender address (recvfrom). A LOCATION whose host
is not the sender's IP is ignored (HandleLocation), so a device on the LAN
cannot make the plugin fetch from arbitrary hosts or announce a server on
behalf of another one. The test starts a "rogue" announcement from 127.0.0.1
pointing to 127.0.0.2 (tests/test_discover.cpp, fake_upnp.py listens on all
addresses).

8. Building and testing
-----------------------
The plugin builds like any VDR plugin (make in PLUGINS/src/vtsatip with the VDR
sources next to it). Without VDR installed:

  tests/run_tests.sh     compiles everything against tests/stubs/vdr/* and
                         runs: config parser, section filters, tuner against
                         tests/fake_satip.py, CI flow, quirk modes
                         (zeros nodelta notune busy mcast nomcast refusemcast
                         tcp notcp refusetcp allpids lose stall), UPnP discovery and
                         the translation check
  tests/syntax_check.sh  compile check of all sources with the stub headers

  tests/build_vdr.sh TAG builds the plugin against the real headers of one VDR
                         release (clones it from GitHub, e.g. tests/build_vdr.sh
                         2.8.3); compiler warnings fail the build and, if
                         gettext is installed, the .mo files are built with
                         msgfmt -c

The stubs are not VDR: they only make the plugin compile. Always build against
the real headers too (VDR >= 2.4).

Continuous integration (.github/workflows/ci.yml): every pull request and every
push to main runs the tests and translation checks (check_po.py and msgfmt -c)
and builds the plugin against VDR 2.4.8, 2.6.9, 2.7.9 and 2.8.3. Add a release
to the matrix there when a new VDR version comes out.

Adding a setup option: common.h (field and default), vtconfig.c (SetupParse),
setup.c/.h (menu item and Store), po/de_DE.po (texts), README, tests/test_cfg.cpp.
Texts shown in the OSD go through tr(); tests/check_po.py fails if a text is
missing in po/de_DE.po. Log messages and SVDRP replies stay English.

9. Where to look when something is wrong
----------------------------------------
  * -t 1 (or Tracing in the setup menu) logs every RTSP request and response and
    each tuning decision to the syslog.
  * "svdrpsend plug vtsatip INFO" shows servers (busy/free) and per device
    session, lock, frontend, signal, rtp=/lost=/reordered=/rate=/gap=/gapmax= and the last params.
  * The statistics page in the setup menu shows the same live.
  * lost= growing with UDP: raise net.core.rmem_max and -r; with -T tcp
    lost stays 0 but latency can grow.
